  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Given that $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ is a solution to the equation $3{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+8x-2=0,$ find the remaining solutions.",
  "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=9{x}^{3}-21{x}^{2}+24x-6.$ Since $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ is a root of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem $(x-\\frac{1}{3})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nWe find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x-\\frac{1}{3})$ using synthetic division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\enclose{bottom right}{\\frac{1}{3}} & 3 & -7 & 8 & -2 \\\\  &  & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\  & 3 & -6 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=(x-\\frac{1}{3})(3{x}^{2}-6x+6).$ \n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve $3{x}^{2}-6x+6=0$ by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6x+6 & =0 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-2x+2) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+2 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =(\\text{i})^{2} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=\\frac{1}{3},\\,1\\pm \\text{i}.$",
